  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q17-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "17",
    "prompt": "A train takes 5 minutes to pass through a tunnel that is $600\\text{ m}$ long. A second train, twice as long as the first, takes 6 minutes to pass through the same tunnel. The trains are travelling at the same speed. Find the length of the smaller train, in metres.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$150\\text{ m}$.\n\nFor each train, the distance travelled while passing completely through the tunnel is the tunnel length plus the train length.",
      "worked": "Let $L\\text{ m}$ be the smaller train's length. The trains travel $(600+L)\\text{ m}$ and $(600+2L)\\text{ m}$ respectively while passing completely through the tunnel. Their speeds are equal.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{600+L}{5}&=\\frac{600+2L}{6}\\\\6(600+L)&=5(600+2L)\\\\3600+6L&=3000+10L\\\\600&=4L\\\\L&=150\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe smaller train is $150\\text{ m}$ long.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 76
  },
